# Webflow → Astro. Every URL the live site publishes, pointed at its new home.
#
# Cloudflare takes the FIRST match, so specific rules sit above the wildcards
# that back them up.
#
# Checked against three sources, which agree: the live sitemap (28 URLs), a
# crawl of every internal link on the live site (19 URLs, no new ones), and
# Bing Webmaster's page report (8 URLs with impressions, no new ones). Every
# known live URL resolves to a page that exists in this build; /contact is the
# only one with no rule, because the path did not change.
#
# Checked against Search Console's own page list (41 URLs, 16 months), which
# earned its place: it found three URLs the sitemap, the crawl, Bing and the
# archive had all missed between them. Two were URL shapes nobody links to —
# a bare /marketing-services and a trailing-slash /b2b-marketing-services/ —
# and one was a second spelling of a live post's slug, carrying 973
# impressions and twelve clicks, that the wildcard was quietly sending to a
# page that does not exist.

# ── Pages that changed name ──
/about-me                    /about                        301
/blogs-page                  /journal                      301
/privacy-policy              /privacy                      301
/b2b-marketing-services      /services                     301
/aberdeen-marketing-agency   /digital-marketing-aberdeen   301

# ── Services left /marketing-services/ ──
# Two live URLs carry the strategy page: its own, and the one the homepage
# labels "Marketing Leadership". Both land on strategy — the new Leadership
# service is a different offer, and sending traffic there would be a guess.
/marketing-services/marketing-strategy                    /services/strategy       301
/marketing-services/digital-marketing-strategy-aberdeen   /services/strategy       301
/marketing-services/marketing-positioning                 /services/positioning    301
/marketing-services/seo-aberdeen                          /services/seo-aberdeen   301
/marketing-services/seo-agency-aberdeen                   /services/seo-aberdeen   301
/marketing-services/digital-marketing                     /digital-marketing-aberdeen 301
/marketing-services/*                                     /services                301
# Indexed without the trailing segment, so the wildcard above never sees them.
/marketing-services                                       /services                301
/b2b-marketing-services/                                  /services                301

# ── Renamed inside this build ──
# Paid Media became Organic & Paid Media, and the slug moved with it. Nothing
# external points here yet, but the old URL has been in this repo for months
# and costs one line to keep working.
/services/paid   /services/media   301

# ── Case studies ──
# /case-study-arnlea is the pre-/proof/ URL and is STILL LIVE — the sitemap
# does not list it, nothing on the site links to it, and only the archive knew
# it existed. Its siblings under that prefix all 404 now, but the wildcard
# catches any that are still indexed.
/case-study-arnlea            /work/arnlea      301
/case-study-*                 /work             301
/proof/case-study-arnlea      /work/arnlea      301
/proof/case-study-viewport3   /work/viewport3   301
/proof/*                      /work             301

# ── Gone before the migration ──
# /resources 404s on the live site already and predates the current structure.
# Whatever it held, the people who followed a link to it wanted reading, so it
# goes to the journal rather than the homepage.
/resources                    /journal          301

# ── Journal ──
# Twelve of the fourteen slugs survive the move unchanged, so the wildcard at
# the foot carries them. These three do not:
#   · the live slug lost its year
#   · a post whose title changed between platforms
#   · the automation post, deleted with the service — no equivalent page, so
#     it goes to the index rather than to something it isn't
/post/seo-aberdeen-the-basics-that-still-win   /journal/seo-aberdeen-the-basics-that-still-win-in-2025   301
# Two spellings of the Base44 slug are indexed: 80m and 80-m. Only the first
# exists here, and the second is the one with the traffic.
/post/b2b-saas-growth-playbook---lessons-from-base44s-80-m-exit   /journal/b2b-saas-growth-playbook---lessons-from-base44s-80m-exit   301
/post/why-every-sme-need-a-marketing-plan      /journal/free-b2b-marketing-plan-template-for-smes        301
/post/b2b-sme-growth-marketing-automation      /journal                                                  301
/post/*                                        /journal/:splat                                           301
